<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/AmountOfSubstance> a <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityKind>;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Jumlah bahan"@ms, "Látkové množství"@cs, "Stoffmenge"@de, "amount of substance"@en,
    "anyagmennyiség"@hu, "cantidad de sustancia"@es, "cantitate de substanță"@ro, "liczność materii"@pl,
    "madde miktarı"@tr, "množina snovi"@sl, "quantidade de substância"@pt, "quantitas substantiae"@la,
    "quantità di sostanza"@it, "quantité de matière"@fr, "Ποσότητα Ουσίας"@el, "Количество вещества"@ru,
    "Количество вещество"@bg, "כמות חומר"@he, "كمية المادة"@ar, "مقدار ماده"@fa, "पदार्थ की मात्रा"@hi,
    "物質量"@ja, "物质的量"@zh;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:AmountOfSubstance";
  dct:description """
  $\\textit{Amount of Substance}$ is a standards-defined quantity that measures the size of an ensemble of elementary entities,
   such as atoms, molecules, electrons, and other particles. 
  It is sometimes referred to as chemical amount.

  The International System of Units (SI) defines the amount of substance to be proportional to the number of elementary entities present. 
  The SI unit for amount of substance is mole. 
  It has the unit symbol $mol$.

  The mole is defined as the amount of substance that contains an equal number of elementary entities as there are atoms in 0.012kg of the isotope carbon-12. 
  This number is called Avogadro's number and has the value $6.02214179(30) \\times 10^{23}$. 
  
  The only other unit of amount of substance in current use is the $pound-mole$ with the symbol $lb-mol$,
   which is sometimes used in chemical engineering in the United States. 
  One $pound-mole$ is exactly $453.59237 mol$.
  """^^<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString>;
  skos:altLabel "chemical amount"@en, "jumlah kimia"@ms, "quantità chimica"@it, "quantità di materia"@it;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/dbpediaMatch> "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Amount_of_substance"^^xsd:anyURI;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/hasDimensionVector> <http://qudt.org/vocab/dimensionvector/A1E0L0I0M0H0T0D0>;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/iec61360Code> "0112/2///62720#UAD004";
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/informativeReference> "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amount_of_substance"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://cdd.iec.ch/cdd/iec61987/iec61987.nsf/ListsOfUnitsAllVersions/0112-2---62720%23UAD004"^^xsd:anyURI;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/siExactMatch> <https://si-digital-framework.org/SI/quantities/AMSU>;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/symbol> "n";
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/isoNormativeReference> "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/applicableUnit> unit:CentiMOL, unit:FemtoMOL, unit:IU,
    unit:KiloMOL, unit:MOL, unit:MOL_LB, unit:MicroMOL, unit:MilliMOL, unit:NanoMOL, unit:OSM,
    unit:PicoMOL, unit:SCF, unit:SCM .

<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/ExtentOfReaction> a <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityKind>;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Extent of Reaction"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ExtentOfReaction";
  dct:description "In physical chemistry, the \"Extent of Reaction\" is a quantity that measures the extent in which the reaction proceeds."^^<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString>;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/hasDimensionVector> <http://qudt.org/vocab/dimensionvector/A1E0L0I0M0H0T0D0>;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/informativeReference> "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extent_of_reaction"^^xsd:anyURI;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/isoNormativeReference> "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/plainTextDescription> "In physical chemistry, the \"Extent of Reaction\" is a quantity that measures the extent in which the reaction proceeds.";
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/latexSymbol> "$\\xi$"^^<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString>;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/latexDefinition> "$dn_B = \\nu_B d\\xi$, where $n_B$ is the amount of substance $B$ and $\\nu_B$ is the stoichiometric number of substance $B$."^^<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString>;
  <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/applicableUnit> unit:CentiMOL, unit:FemtoMOL, unit:MOL,
    unit:NanoMOL, unit:PicoMOL .

